WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.940
Every time a mouse turns left, one hidden neuron fires.

2
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.320
How can you watch that living signal, and test whether it causes the turn?

3
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.940
Signals inside cells are tiny and fast.

4
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.800
Engineered proteins let you answer both questions with light.

5
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.710
Two kinds of protein do the work.

6
00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:28.670
A reporter turns a cell's signal into light so you can watch it live.

7
00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.690
An actuator works the other way.

8
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:36.490
It turns a flash of light or a drug into a signal on command.

9
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.360
Reporters start with green fluorescent protein from a jellyfish.

10
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:43.900
How does fluorescence work?

11
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.580
A molecule absorbs a blue photon and lifts an electron.

12
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.840
A little energy leaves as heat before a green photon comes out.

13
00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.760
So light leaves at a longer wavelength than it entered.

14
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.200
Green fluorescent protein folds into a tight barrel with its glowing center shielded

15
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.900
inside. Under blue light, that sealed barrel glows steadily.

16
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:15.160
To make brightness track calcium, engineers cut a narrow slit beside that glowing

17
00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:19.220
center. Water slips through the slit and dims the glow.

18
00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:26.143
Next, engineers attach a calcium detector called calmodulin to one edge, and a

19
00:01:26.143 --> 00:01:27.603
partner peptide to the other.

20
00:01:28.843 --> 00:01:30.683
That hybrid sensor is GCaMP.

21
00:01:31.923 --> 00:01:36.223
When calcium enters, calmodulin grabs it and wraps around the peptide.

22
00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:41.103
Together they plug the slit, shut water out, and brighten the barrel.

23
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:46.837
Every time the mouse turns left, this GCaMP neuron flashes bright green.

24
00:01:48.357 --> 00:01:54.457
Pause and think. Does that flash prove firing this neuron causes the turn, or could

25
00:01:54.457 --> 00:01:55.857
another circuit drive both?

26
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:06.910
Watching alone cannot prove causation, because another brain circuit could drive

27
00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:09.910
both the turn and the calcium flash at once.

28
00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.650
So to test causation, you cannot only watch.

29
00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:18.070
You must push this neuron yourself and see what the mouse does.

30
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:20.713
That takes an actuator.

31
00:02:22.053 --> 00:02:25.133
Meet channelrhodopsin-2, borrowed from a green alga.

32
00:02:26.353 --> 00:02:31.333
Like rhodopsin in your eye, it holds light-sensing retinal inside seven membrane

33
00:02:31.333 --> 00:02:38.423
helices. Your eye's protein then passes the message to a G protein, a go-between.

34
00:02:38.423 --> 00:02:43.433
Channelrhodopsin-2 skips that go-between: it is itself an ion pore.

35
00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:46.467
Flash blue light onto the membrane.

36
00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:50.907
Retinal shifts shape, and the pore opens within milliseconds.

37
00:02:52.527 --> 00:02:56.687
Cations rush in, and their positive charge makes the inside less negative.

38
00:02:57.907 --> 00:03:03.007
The voltage climbs past the firing threshold, the neuron fires, and the mouse turns

39
00:03:03.007 --> 00:03:04.007
left.

40
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:09.290
Both tools are engineered proteins that living cells can build from a gene.

41
00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:13.050
Put them together and you hold the first rule.

42
00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:18.610
Reporters turn signals into light, and actuators control them on command.
